Subject: [PATCH] cron: make skip_memory opt-in rather than hardcoded

Upstream hardcodes skip_memory=True for cron-initiated agent runs, reasoning
that cron system prompts would corrupt user representations. Backbone wants the
opposite: cron agents read and write persistent memory, gated behind an explicit
config flag.

Enable with, in config.yaml:

    cron:
      memory_enabled: true

Discovered 2026-07-25 as an UNCOMMITTED modification in the running checkout,
which the Phase 0 audit missed. Without it all 8 cron jobs run with no memory
access, and the failure is silent: no crash, no failed probe, just worse agents.
See docs/OPERATIONS.md.

Verified to apply cleanly against a fresh clone of v2026.7.1.

diff --git a/cron/scheduler.py b/cron/scheduler.py
index 4c764bd13a..3183ff259c 100644
--- a/cron/scheduler.py
+++ b/cron/scheduler.py
@@ -2824,7 +2824,13 @@ def run_job(job: dict) -> tuple[bool, str, str, Optional[str]]:
             # Without a workdir, keep cwd context discovery disabled.
             skip_context_files=not bool(_job_workdir),
             load_soul_identity=True,
-            skip_memory=True,  # Cron system prompts would corrupt user representations
+            # Local patch (reapplied 2026-07-02 after v0.18.0 update): upstream
+            # hardcodes skip_memory=True here. Sheldon wants cron agents to
+            # read/write persistent memory (hybrid provider + MEMORY.md), so
+            # it's opt-in via `cron: memory_enabled: true` in config.yaml.
+            # A hermes update reverts this file — reapply if cron runs start
+            # logging "Memory is not available".
+            skip_memory=not bool((_cfg.get("cron") or {}).get("memory_enabled", False)),
             platform="cron",
             session_id=_cron_session_id,
             session_db=_session_db,
